Organic-inorganic hybrid perovskite solar cells (PSCs) are considered as promising competitors to silicon solar cells because of their unprecedented power conversion efficiency (PCE) as high as $22.7\%$ under one sun illumination.[1-8] However, the primary challenge to drag their commercialization is poor moisture-tolerance of hybrid perovskite-structured harvestors. Therefore, the fabrication and storage processes of state-of-theart PSCs are always performed in inert gases or low-humid conditions.[9,10] Recently, some advanced techniques by isolating water-stream or stabilizing crystal lattices have been conceived to withstand moisture for prolonging device lifetimes.[11,12] Another strategy is to replace an organic counterpart with a cesium ion $(\mathsf{C}\mathsf{s}^{+})$ and to remove hydrophilic hole-transporting layers (HTLs) for all-inorganic HTL-free PSCs with classical $\mathsf{F T O}/\mathsf{T i O}_{2}/\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}/($ carbon configuration. The major superiority of these photovoltaics is the enhanced chemical stability.[13-15] Although the inorganic CsPbBr3 crystal has a good humidity tolerance, the corresponding solar cells still suffer performance attenuation in high humidity, not to mention multi-energy harvesting from sun and atmosphere environments. Another problem in chemistry for perovskite CsPbBr3 is wide bandgap of
