$2.3~\mathsf{e V}$ , allowing for large energy differences at $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ (-4.2 eV)/CsPbBr3 $(-3.3~\mathsf{e V})$ and CsPbBr3 (-5.6 eV)/carbon $(-5.0~\mathsf{e V})$ interfaces. Therefore, the sluggish charge extraction processes produce serious electron-hole recombination at interfaces. By setting intermediate energy levels at cell interfaces with quantum dots, the photo-induced charges may be significantly separated under local electric field without destroying interface structures.
Additionally, a commonly used method to improve solar cell stability is to avoid moisture attack to CsPbBr3 layer, arising from the impossible co-existence of perovskite light-harvestor and moisture, not to mention converting water-stream energy into electricity. In this work, we pioneer a new route of making carbon electrode tailored all-inorganic PSCs to simultaneously harvest solar and water-stream energies, aiming at enhancing overall power output of a solar cell. Two main reasons are considered to address this work: (i) Proper moisture can optimize perovskite crystallization;[16,17] (ii) The flowing water steam within nanoporous carbonaceous materials can create potentials. Through interfacial engineering, graphene quantum dots (GQDs) are used to modify $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}/\mathsf{C s P b}\mathsf{B r}_{3}$ interface and ternary CuInS2/ZnS alloy quantum dots (CISZ-QDs) are for hole extraction at CsPbBr3/carbon interface. As shown in Figure 1, the bifunctional all-inorganic PSC is made by successively coating a layer of compact $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ $\left(c{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\right)$ , mesoscopic $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ (m$\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\mathsf{\backslash}$ and CsPbBr3 as well as a carbon electrode. The experimental details are found in the Supporting Information. We focus on studying charge extraction for PCE enhancement, water-stream energy harvest by this PSC, and improved solar cell stability in high humidity.
The integration of different energy conversion mechanisms into a single solar cell device can maximize energy harvesting from nature. Undoubtedly, solar energy is converted into electricity through photoelectric principle. Upon sun illumination,
CsPbBr3 layer absorbs photons and releases electron-hole pairs, which are extracted by $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ and carbon under internal electrical field, respectively. Simultaneously, the vapour from water evaporation penetrates through the carbon back (CB)-electrode for flowing potentials. According to previous reports, the negative zeta potential of CB layer can create repulsion to hydroxyl ions $(\mathsf{O H\bar{\cdot}})$ and adsorb protons $(\mathsf{H}^{+})$ to form electron (CB)/proton (water stream) electrical double layer (EDL) at CB/water interfaces.[18,19] Therefore, the full-carbonaceous backelectrode is responsible to both convert water-stream energy into electricity and to extract holes from $\mathsf{C s P b}\mathsf{B r}_{3}$ layer, and the power output of each sub-device determines the total performances of this bifunctional solar cell.
In experimental, CsPbBr3 film with large grain size up to 1 μm is fabricated onto $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ surface by a multi-step solutionprocessed technology proposed by our group (see Figure 2a and XRD characterization in Figure S1), the high purity is beneficial for reducing recombination at grain boundaries.[15] The top-view scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image of asprepared CB-electrode shows that the carbonaceous film is made of enormous particles with average size of around $50~\mathsf{n m}$ and interconnected channels for flowing water stream. Figure 2c displays a layered structure of the all-inorganic PSC device having $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ , vertical CsPbBr3 and carbon electrode with thickness of $200\ \mathrm{nm},\ 300$ nm and $15\ \upmu\mathrm{m},$ respectively. The photocurrent-voltage $(J-V)$ curve of this solar cell is presented in Figure 2d, yielding a PCE of $7.76\%$ with short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathsf{s c}})$ of $6.86~\mathsf{m A}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{{oc}}})$ of 1.438 $\vee$ and fill factor $(F F)$ of $78.6\%$ under one sun irradiation. The PCE is still much lower than that of state-of-the-art hybrid PSCs owing to larger energy barriers at interfaces. In order to improve the device performance, GQDs and CISZ-QDs are inserted into $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}/\mathsf{C s P b}\mathsf{B r}_{3}$ and CsPbBr3/carbon interfaces for promoting electron and hole extraction, respectively. The optimal device achieves a champion PCE as high as $9.43\%$ as shown in Figure 2d,e (the corresponding photovoltaic characterizations including IPCE spectra, stable power output are provided in Figure S2), this arises from the accelerated charge extraction ability owing to matchable energy level alignments (Figure S3).[20,21] The detailed photovoltaic data are summarized in Table 1. Furthermore, the CISZ-QDs decorated with hydrophobic long alkyl chains can protect CsPbBr3 from dissolution, ensuring the co-existance of perovskite and moisture.[22]
In actual environment, the humid ground keeps evaporation day and night, and water stream penetrates through the backward carbon electrode, as shown in Figure 1. Therefore, the integration of harvesting solar and water-stream energies in a single solar cell device is practically significant. A horizontal baseline for CB-only film suggests no electricity generation owing to its excellent hydrophobic property as shown in Figure $\mathsf{S4}$ . In this fashion, the carbon layer can also act as an isolated layer for high-stable PSCs.[23,24] Prior to electrical measurement upon persistent humidification by $80\%$ RH at room temperature, the CB-electrode is plasma treated for different times. In order to optimize power output, hydrophilic GOs are introduced into the carbon film, yielding a stable voltage of $0.03\mathrm{~V~}$ and current of $0.02~\upmu\mathsf{A}$ free of plasma treatment (Figure 2f,g). The relative low electrical signals result from inferior hydrophilic property. When treated by plasma technique to introduce abundant hydrophilic sites for increased steam flowing driven by water capillary action,[25] the carbon electrode achieves maximum voltage and current up to $0.35~\mathsf{V}$ and $0.45~\upmu\mathsf{A}.$ , as shown in Figure 2f,g. A reasonable mechanism behind this phenomenon is the introduction of hydrophilic oxygenic groups such as -COOH, $c=0$ , C-O-C etc and enhancement of electron depletion in EDL. Once the plasma-processing time is prolonged beyond 7 min, the conjugated structures in carbonaceous materials may be destroyed. According to the power generation principle in chemistry, the reduced electron concentration leads to weak EDL and therefore significant degradation of electrical signals.[26] The above-mentioned conclusion can be cross-checked by Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy and contact angle characterizations, as shown in Figure S5. In addition, the plasma treatment can only introduce hydrophilic oxygenic groups on the surface-area of carbon film without changing the energy level and charge extraction of carbon electrode from solar cell side. Notably, tens of seconds are required for electrical signal climbout to their maximal values and subsequently keep constants. Therefore this is a prewetting process along with the evaporation of water-stream, resulting complete formation of EDL layers at CB/water-stream interfaces.
According to thermodynamic process, the water stream with higher humidity may produce concentrated protons to form highcapacitance EDL, stemming from enhanced voltage and current generation. Therefore, the dependence of electrical signals on humidity is studied on so-called bifunctional PSC. As shown in Figure 3a,b, the electrical signals of both voltage and current are highly dependent on humidity, intriguingly, the values increase stepwise along with the pumping high-humidity water-stream into the carbon layer. When the humidity is $<20\%$ RH, both voltage and current values are nearly zero, which is mainly attributed to the unequilibrium of water-stream adsorption and volatilization on the film surface and therefore the water-stream can not permeate into the interior of carbon film to form EDL layers. On the contrary, the induced voltage rises to 0.05, 0.17, 0.30 and $0.35\mathrm{~V~}$ by increasing humidity to $50\%$ , $65\%$ , $75\%$ , and $80\%$ RH, while the current goes up to 0.17, 0.24, 0.33 and 0.36 $\upmu\mathsf{A}$ , respectively. The preliminary results suggest that the high humidity water stream can create larger pressure and accelerate the migration of water-stream, leading to a charge gradients at a fixed temperature. This similar phenomenon has also been previously found by other groups.[27,28] High efficiency and improved stability have equal significance in assessing practical applications of an energy conversion device. As shown in Figure 3c,d, the solar cell can export nearly unchanged voltage of ${\sim}0.4$ V and current of ${\sim}0.3~\mathsf{\mu A}$ under continuously humidification by $80\%$ RH water-stream over eight hours.
It has been demonstrated that this carbon electrode tailored all-inorganic PSC achieves high-efficiency conversion from solar and water-stream energies into electricity. How is the long-term stability of CsPbBr3 film and photovoltaic performance in moisture? UV-vis absorption spectra of CsPbBr3 film exposed to $80\%$ RH air are recorded to study crystal structure change, as shown in Figure 4a. Obviously, $\mathsf{C s P b B r_{3}}$ absorbs light with wavelength shorter than ${\sim}540~\mathsf{n m}$ and presents a characteristic perks centered at $515\ \mathsf{n m}$ , which is in an agreement with previous reports.[13,15,29] Even under persistent exposure to $80\%$ humidity for eight hours, the absorption peak positions and intensities are nearly unchanged, showing an excellent moisture tolerance in comparison with organic-inorganic hybrid perovskite harvestor such as CH3NH3PbI3, in which the hybrid perovskite crystal degrades into lead halide quickly under $80\%$ RH.[30] In order to better quantify the decomposition rate, the absorbance at $515~\mathsf{n m}$ is plotted against exposure time, as shown in Figure 4b, a linear fit of this date suggests that the half-life period of this CsPbBr3 film is nearly 500 hours. Finally, we perform the normalized performances of the solar cell upon persistent attack by flowing water-stream ( $80\%$ RH, $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ ) without encapsulation. As shown in Figure $4\mathsf{c}\mathsf{-}4\mathsf{f},$ all the photovoltaic parameters including PCE, $J_{\mathsf{s c}}$ , $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ , and $F F$ display remarkable stability with $98\%$ of initial efficiency over 40 days owing to the stabilized lattice structure by smaller sized Br- as well as the hydrophobic protection layers on the $\mathsf{C s P b B r_{3}}$ surface.[31,32] All of these results are highly encouraging to the perspective of carbon electrode tailored all-inorganic PSCs in high-humidity conditions.
In summary, we present here experimental realization of physical proof-of-concept bifunctional all-inorganic PSCs to simultaneously harvest solar and water-stream energies, making them become “friends” from “enemies”. An unprecedented PCE as high as $9.43\%$ under solar irradiation and a power output of $0.158~\upmu\mathrm{w}$ with voltage of $0.35\mathrm{~V~}$ and current of $0.45~\upmu\mathsf{A}$ induced by water-stream are achieved in the newly launched solar cell device. The advantages of high-efficiency, improved stability, and simultaneously harvest for solar and water-stream energies provide new insights into multifunctional photovoltaics.
